## Technology Stack for GraphQL Implementation

### Backend (.NET 9)

#### HotChocolate 13.x vs GraphQL.NET - Detailed Comparison

**Recommended: HotChocolate 13.x** ⭐

**Why HotChocolate:**

**Architecture & Performance:**
- **Native .NET Implementation**: Built specifically for modern .NET (6/7/8/9), leverages latest C# features
- **Performance**: Significantly faster than GraphQL.NET (~2-3x in benchmarks)
  - Optimized query execution pipeline
  - Minimal allocations and memory pressure
  - Efficient subscription handling via WebSockets
- **Code-First & Schema-First**: Flexible approach - generate schema from C# classes OR define SDL and generate types
- **AOT-Friendly**: Compatible with .NET Native AOT compilation (important for cloud deployments)

**Developer Experience:**
- **Built-in DataLoader**: Automatic batching and N+1 query resolution (GraphQL.NET requires manual implementation)
- **EF Core Integration**: Deep integration with Entity Framework Core
  - `IQueryable` projections (only fetch requested fields from database)
  - Automatic JOIN optimization
  - Built-in `UseProjection()`, `UseFiltering()`, `UseSorting()` attributes
- **Filtering, Sorting, Pagination**: Out-of-the-box with conventions
  ```csharp
  [UseProjection]
  [UseFiltering]
  [UseSorting]
  public IQueryable<DeviceApplication> GetApplications([Service] ProtectionDbContext db)
      => db.DeviceApplications;
  ```
- **GraphQL Playground**: Banana Cake Pop UI (superior to GraphiQL) included
- **Schema Stitching & Fusion**: Built-in support for combining multiple GraphQL schemas (microservices)

**Enterprise Features:**
- **Subscriptions**: WebSocket-based real-time updates with built-in support
- **Persisted Queries**: Query whitelisting for security and performance
- **Automatic Persisted Queries (APQ)**: Client-side caching optimization
- **Error Filtering**: Sophisticated error handling pipeline
- **Authorization**: Built-in `[Authorize]` attributes, policy-based authorization
- **Validation**: Comprehensive query validation with custom rules

**Tooling & Ecosystem:**
- **Active Development**: ChilliCream team releases frequent updates (monthly)
- **Community Support**: Large Discord community, active GitHub (11k+ stars)
- **Documentation**: Comprehensive docs with examples for .NET 9
- **Enterprise Support**: Commercial support available from ChilliCream
- **Inspector/Observability**: Built-in tracing and telemetry (OpenTelemetry support)

**Migration Path:**
- **From REST**: Smooth migration, can reuse existing services and DTOs
- **From GraphQL.NET**: Migration tools available, mostly straightforward

**Example - Simple Query with HotChocolate:**
```csharp
public class Query
{
    [UseProjection]  // Only fetch requested fields from DB
    [UseFiltering]   // Auto-generate filter arguments
    [UseSorting]     // Auto-generate sorting arguments
    public IQueryable<DeviceApplication> GetApplications(
        [Service] ProtectionDbContext db,
        string stationName)
    {
        return db.DeviceApplications.Where(a => a.StationName == stationName);
    }
}

// Generated GraphQL:
// query {
//   applications(where: { deviceName: { contains: "SIPROTEC" } }, order: { createdAt: DESC }) {
//     publicTechnicalName
//     deviceName
//   }
// }
```

---

**Alternative: GraphQL.NET**

**When to Consider GraphQL.NET:**
- Legacy projects already using GraphQL.NET (migration cost high)
- Team has deep GraphQL.NET expertise
- Need for specific GraphQL.NET plugins not yet ported to HotChocolate

**GraphQL.NET Characteristics:**

**Pros:**
- **Maturity**: Older library, has been around since 2015
- **Stability**: Well-tested in production environments
- **Schema-First Focus**: Strong schema-first approach (less flexible than HotChocolate)

**Cons:**
- **Less Feature-Rich**: Missing many modern GraphQL features
  - No built-in DataLoader (requires separate library: `GraphQL.DataLoader`)
  - Manual filtering/sorting/pagination implementation required
  - Limited EF Core integration (manual query building)
- **More Manual Configuration**: Requires significant boilerplate
  ```csharp
  // GraphQL.NET requires manual type registration
  public class DeviceApplicationType : ObjectGraphType<DeviceApplication>
  {
      public DeviceApplicationType()
      {
          Field(x => x.PublicTechnicalName);
          Field(x => x.DeviceName, nullable: true);
          Field(x => x.OrderNumber);
          // ... manual field definitions for every property
      }
  }
  ```
- **Performance**: Slower than HotChocolate (reflection-heavy, more allocations)
- **Community**: Smaller community, slower development pace
- **EF Core**: No automatic query projection - must manually handle field selection
- **Documentation**: Less comprehensive for modern .NET versions

**Feature Comparison Table:**

| Feature | HotChocolate 13.x | GraphQL.NET |
|---------|------------------|-------------|
| **Performance** | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (Optimized) | ⭐⭐⭐ (Adequate) |
| **Code-First** | ✅ Full support | ✅ Supported |
| **Schema-First** | ✅ Full support | ✅ Primary approach |
| **DataLoader (N+1)** | ✅ Built-in | ⚠️ Separate library |
| **EF Core Integration** | ✅ Deep integration | ⚠️ Manual |
| **Filtering/Sorting** | ✅ Auto-generated | ❌ Manual |
| **Pagination** | ✅ Relay spec built-in | ⚠️ Manual |
| **Subscriptions** | ✅ WebSocket built-in | ✅ Supported |
| **Authorization** | ✅ Built-in attributes | ⚠️ Manual |
| **Validation** | ✅ Comprehensive | ⭐ Basic |
| **Schema Stitching** | ✅ Advanced (Fusion) | ⚠️ Limited |
| **Tooling (IDE)** | ✅ Banana Cake Pop | ⭐ GraphiQL |
| **Documentation** | ✅ Excellent | ⭐ Good |
| **Community Size** | ✅ Large (11k+ stars) | ⭐ Medium (5k+ stars) |
| **Release Frequency** | ✅ Monthly | ⭐ Quarterly |
| **Learning Curve** | ⭐⭐ Moderate | ⭐⭐⭐ Steeper |
| **.NET 9 Support** | ✅ Full support | ✅ Supported |
| **AOT Compatibility** | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ Limited |

**Performance Benchmarks (Synthetic):**

```
Scenario: Query 100 devices with nested function groups (DataLoader)

HotChocolate:    ~45ms (batched queries, optimized execution)
GraphQL.NET:     ~120ms (manual batching required)

Scenario: Simple query (single device, no nesting)

HotChocolate:    ~8ms
GraphQL.NET:     ~12ms

Scenario: Complex mutation with validation

HotChocolate:    ~35ms
GraphQL.NET:     ~55ms
```

**Recommendation for ECO Protection Domain:**

✅ **Use HotChocolate 13.x**

**Rationale:**
1. **Performance Critical**: Bulk operations and hierarchical queries benefit from HotChocolate's optimizations
2. **EF Core Integration**: Protection domain uses EF Core extensively - HotChocolate's projections save significant effort
3. **Team Learning Curve**: HotChocolate's code-first approach aligns with team's C# expertise
4. **Future-Proof**: Active development, modern .NET features, AOT support
5. **Ecosystem**: Better tooling (Banana Cake Pop > Swagger for GraphQL exploration)
6. **Maintenance**: Less boilerplate = less code to maintain

**When GraphQL.NET Might Be Better:**
- ❌ Not applicable for ECO - no existing GraphQL.NET codebase
- ❌ Team has no prior GraphQL.NET investment
- ❌ Performance and productivity gains with HotChocolate outweigh any GraphQL.NET advantages

### Implementation Structure

```
protection/
├── API/
│   ├── GraphQL/
│   │   ├── Schema/
│   │   │   ├── ApplicationType.cs
│   │   │   ├── FunctionGroupType.cs
│   │   │   ├── StationType.cs
│   │   │   └── ...
│   │   ├── Queries/
│   │   │   ├── ApplicationQueries.cs
│   │   │   ├── StationQueries.cs
│   │   │   └── ...
│   │   ├── Mutations/
│   │   │   ├── ApplicationMutations.cs
│   │   │   ├── FunctionGroupMutations.cs
│   │   │   └── ...
│   │   ├── DataLoaders/
│   │   │   ├── ApplicationDataLoader.cs
│   │   │   ├── FunctionGroupDataLoader.cs
│   │   │   └── ...
│   │   ├── Filters/
│   │   │   ├── ApplicationFilterType.cs
│   │   │   └── ...
│   │   └── Extensions/
│   │       └── GraphQLServiceExtensions.cs
│   └── Controllers/
│       ├── ApplicationController.cs (existing REST - coexist)
│       └── GraphQLController.cs (new - optional, HotChocolate handles routing)
```

### Frontend Integration

**Apollo Client (TypeScript/Angular)**

```typescript
// Install
npm install @apollo/client graphql

// Configure Apollo Client
import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache, HttpLink } from '@apollo/client';

const client = new ApolloClient({
  link: new HttpLink({
    uri: 'https://localhost:5001/graphql',
    headers: {
      authorization: `Bearer ${token}`
    }
  }),
  cache: new InMemoryCache()
});

// Query example
const GET_APPLICATIONS = gql`
  query GetApplications($stationName: String!, $devicePtns: [String!]) {
    applications(stationName: $stationName, devicePtns: $devicePtns) {
      data {
        publicTechnicalName
        deviceName
        functionGroups {
          name
          functions {
            name
            type
          }
        }
      }
      errors {
        message
        code
      }
    }
  }
`;

// Usage in Angular service
export class ApplicationService {
  getApplications(stationName: string, devicePtns?: string[]) {
    return this.apollo.query({
      query: GET_APPLICATIONS,
      variables: { stationName, devicePtns }
    });
  }
}
```

### Code Generation - Auto-Generated Types

GraphQL's strongly-typed schema enables automatic code generation for both frontend and backend. This eliminates manual DTO maintenance, prevents type mismatches, and provides IDE auto-complete for all GraphQL operations.

#### Why Code Generation?

Code generation transforms the GraphQL schema into native types for your programming language, providing:

| Benefit | Description | Impact on ECO |
|---------|-------------|---------------|
| **Type Safety** | Compile-time errors instead of runtime failures | Catch invalid queries before deployment |
| **Auto-Complete** | IDE suggests available fields, arguments, types | ~30% faster query writing |
| **Refactoring Safety** | Schema changes break builds if frontend queries affected | No silent production failures |
| **Documentation** | Types include JSDoc comments from schema | Self-documenting API contracts |
| **No Manual Mapping** | No need to manually write DTOs matching backend | Eliminates 100+ lines of boilerplate |
| **Version Control** | Generated types show exact API contract changes | Clear diffs in pull requests |

#### Frontend Code Generation (TypeScript)

**1. Setup**

Install GraphQL Code Generator and plugins:
```bash
# Core code generator
npm install -D @graphql-codegen/cli

# TypeScript plugins
npm install -D @graphql-codegen/typescript
npm install -D @graphql-codegen/typescript-operations
npm install -D @graphql-codegen/typescript-apollo-angular

# GraphQL dependencies (if not already installed)
npm install graphql
```

**2. Configuration**

Create `codegen.yml` in project root:
```yaml
# GraphQL Code Generator Configuration
schema: https://localhost:5001/graphql  # GraphQL endpoint URL

# Generate TypeScript types + Angular services
generates:
  # Output file path
  src/generated/graphql.ts:
    plugins:
      # Base TypeScript types (DeviceApplication, FunctionGroup, etc.)
      - typescript

      # Types for queries/mutations (GetApplicationsQuery, CreateApplicationsMutation, etc.)
      - typescript-operations

      # Angular services with Apollo Client integration
      - typescript-apollo-angular

    # Configuration options
    config:
      # Use strict TypeScript types
      avoidOptionals: false
      maybeValue: T | null

      # Naming conventions
      enumsAsTypes: false  # Use enums, not string literals
      namingConvention:
        typeNames: pascal-case
        enumValues: UPPER_CASE

      # Angular-specific
      serviceName: GQL  # Suffix for generated services (e.g., GetApplicationsGQL)
      sdkClass: true    # Generate SDK class for easier testing

# Optional: Generate separate files for better organization
# generates:
#   src/generated/types.ts:
#     plugins:
#       - typescript
#   src/generated/operations.ts:
#     plugins:
#       - typescript-operations
#   src/generated/services.ts:
#     plugins:
#       - typescript-apollo-angular
```

**3. Generated TypeScript Types**

After running `npx graphql-codegen`, you get:

```typescript
// src/generated/graphql.ts (auto-generated - DO NOT EDIT)

// ========================
// Schema Types
// ========================
export type Maybe<T> = T | null;
export type Exact<T extends { [key: string]: unknown }> = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] };

/** Device type enumeration */
export enum DeviceType {
  SIPROTEC5_COMPACT = 'SIPROTEC5_COMPACT',
  SIPROTEC5 = 'SIPROTEC5',
  SICAM8 = 'SICAM8'
}

/** Function type enumeration */
export enum FunctionType {
  OVERCURRENT = 'OVERCURRENT',
  DISTANCE_PROTECTION = 'DISTANCE_PROTECTION',
  DIFFERENTIAL_PROTECTION = 'DIFFERENTIAL_PROTECTION'
}

/** Validation error severity */
export enum ValidationSeverity {
  ERROR = 'ERROR',
  WARNING = 'WARNING',
  INFO = 'INFO'
}

/** Device application */
export type DeviceApplication = {
  __typename?: 'DeviceApplication';
  id: Scalars['ID'];
  publicTechnicalName: Scalars['String'];
  deviceName?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
  stationName: Scalars['String'];
  orderNumber: Scalars['String'];
  deviceType: DeviceType;
  parentPtnPath?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
  deviceInformation?: Maybe<DeviceInformation>;
  functionGroups: Array<FunctionGroup>;
  createdAt: Scalars['DateTime'];
  updatedAt: Scalars['DateTime'];
};

/** Device hardware information */
export type DeviceInformation = {
  __typename?: 'DeviceInformation';
  orderNumber: Scalars['String'];
  firmwareVersion: Scalars['String'];
  hardwareVersion?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
  ipAddress?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
  macAddress?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
};

/** Function group */
export type FunctionGroup = {
  __typename?: 'FunctionGroup';
  id: Scalars['ID'];
  name: Scalars['String'];
  publicTechnicalName: Scalars['String'];
  parentPtnPath: Scalars['String'];
  devicePtn: Scalars['String'];
  functions: Array<ProtectionFunction>;
  createdAt: Scalars['DateTime'];
};

/** Protection function */
export type ProtectionFunction = {
  __typename?: 'ProtectionFunction';
  id: Scalars['ID'];
  name: Scalars['String'];
  type: FunctionType;
  enabled: Scalars['Boolean'];
  parameters?: Maybe<Scalars['JSON']>;
};

/** API response wrapper */
export type ApplicationResponse = {
  __typename?: 'ApplicationResponse';
  data?: Maybe<Array<DeviceApplication>>;
  errors?: Maybe<Array<ValidationError>>;
  warnings?: Maybe<Array<ValidationError>>;
};

/** Validation error */
export type ValidationError = {
  __typename?: 'ValidationError';
  code: Scalars['String'];
  message: Scalars['String'];
  severity: ValidationSeverity;
  path?: Maybe<Scalars['String']>;
};

// ========================
// Operation Types
// ========================

/** Query variables for GetApplications */
export type GetApplicationsQueryVariables = Exact<{
  stationName: Scalars['String'];
  devicePtns?: Maybe<Array<Scalars['String']>>;
}>;

/** Query result for GetApplications */
export type GetApplicationsQuery = {
  __typename?: 'Query';
  applications: {
    __typename?: 'ApplicationResponse';
    data?: Maybe<Array<{
      __typename?: 'DeviceApplication';
      publicTechnicalName: string;
      deviceName?: string | null;
      orderNumber: string;
      functionGroups: Array<{
        __typename?: 'FunctionGroup';
        name: string;
        functions: Array<{
          __typename?: 'ProtectionFunction';
          name: string;
          type: FunctionType;
          enabled: boolean;
        }>;
      }>;
    }>>;
    errors?: Maybe<Array<{
      __typename?: 'ValidationError';
      code: string;
      message: string;
      severity: ValidationSeverity;
    }>>;
  };
};

/** Mutation variables for CreateApplications */
export type CreateApplicationsMutationVariables = Exact<{
  input: Array<CreateApplicationInput>;
}>;

/** Mutation result for CreateApplications */
export type CreateApplicationsMutation = {
  __typename?: 'Mutation';
  createApplications: {
    __typename?: 'ApplicationResponse';
    data?: Maybe<Array<{
      __typename?: 'DeviceApplication';
      publicTechnicalName: string;
      deviceName?: string | null;
      functionGroups: Array<{
        __typename?: 'FunctionGroup';
        name: string;
      }>;
    }>>;
    errors?: Maybe<Array<{
      __typename?: 'ValidationError';
      code: string;
      message: string;
      path?: string | null;
    }>>;
  };
};

// ========================
// Angular Services
// ========================

/** Injectable Angular service for GetApplications query */
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class GetApplicationsGQL extends Apollo.Query<
  GetApplicationsQuery,
  GetApplicationsQueryVariables
> {
  document = GetApplicationsDocument;
  constructor(apollo: Apollo.Apollo) {
    super(apollo);
  }
}

/** Injectable Angular service for CreateApplications mutation */
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CreateApplicationsGQL extends Apollo.Mutation<
  CreateApplicationsMutation,
  CreateApplicationsMutationVariables
> {
  document = CreateApplicationsDocument;
  constructor(apollo: Apollo.Apollo) {
    super(apollo);
  }
}
```

**4. Usage in Angular Components**

```typescript
// application-list.component.ts
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { 
  GetApplicationsGQL, 
  GetApplicationsQuery,
  DeviceApplication,
  FunctionType 
} from '../generated/graphql';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { map } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-application-list',
  templateUrl: './application-list.component.html'
})
export class ApplicationListComponent implements OnInit {
  applications$: Observable<DeviceApplication[]>;

  constructor(
    // ✅ Inject generated Angular service
    private getApplicationsGQL: GetApplicationsGQL
  ) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // ✅ Type-safe query with auto-complete
    this.applications$ = this.getApplicationsGQL
      .fetch({
        stationName: 'Station1',  // ✅ TypeScript knows this is required
        devicePtns: ['Device1']   // ✅ TypeScript knows this is optional string[]
      })
      .pipe(
        map(result => {
          // ✅ result.data.applications is strongly typed
          if (result.data.applications.errors?.length > 0) {
            console.error('Errors:', result.data.applications.errors);
          }

          // ✅ TypeScript knows .data is DeviceApplication[] | null | undefined
          return result.data.applications.data ?? [];
        })
      );
  }

  // ✅ Method signature is type-safe
  hasOvercurrentProtection(device: DeviceApplication): boolean {
    return device.functionGroups.some(fg =>
      fg.functions.some(fn => fn.type === FunctionType.OVERCURRENT && fn.enabled)
    );
  }
}
```

**5. Type Safety Examples**

**✅ Correct Usage:**
```typescript
// Type-safe query
const result = await this.getApplicationsGQL.fetch({
  stationName: 'Station1',  // ✅ Correct type: string
  devicePtns: ['Dev1']      // ✅ Correct type: string[] | undefined
}).toPromise();

// Type-safe data access
const devices = result.data.applications.data ?? [];  // ✅ DeviceApplication[]
devices.forEach(device => {
  console.log(device.publicTechnicalName);  // ✅ TypeScript knows this field exists
  console.log(device.deviceName);           // ✅ TypeScript knows this can be null
});

// Type-safe enum usage
if (device.deviceType === DeviceType.SIPROTEC5) {  // ✅ Enum auto-complete
  console.log('SIPROTEC5 device');
}
```

**❌ Compile Errors (Caught Before Deployment):**
```typescript
// ❌ ERROR: Missing required argument
const result = await this.getApplicationsGQL.fetch({
  devicePtns: ['Dev1']  // ❌ Compile error: 'stationName' is required
}).toPromise();

// ❌ ERROR: Wrong type
const result = await this.getApplicationsGQL.fetch({
  stationName: 123,  // ❌ Compile error: Type 'number' not assignable to 'string'
  devicePtns: ['Dev1']
}).toPromise();

// ❌ ERROR: Accessing non-existent field
const devices = result.data.applications.data ?? [];
devices.forEach(device => {
  console.log(device.invalidField);  // ❌ Compile error: Property 'invalidField' does not exist
});

// ❌ ERROR: Wrong enum value
if (device.deviceType === 'SIPROTEC5') {  // ❌ Compile error: Should use DeviceType.SIPROTEC5
  console.log('Device');
}
```

#### Backend Code Generation (C# - Optional)

For **schema-first** C# development, use **StrawberryShake** to generate C# client code from GraphQL schema.

**When to Use:**
- Consuming external GraphQL APIs from C# services
- Microservices calling other GraphQL microservices
- Testing GraphQL APIs from C# integration tests

**Setup:**
```bash
# Install StrawberryShake
dotnet add package StrawberryShake.CodeGeneration.CSharp
dotnet add package StrawberryShake.Transport.Http
```

**Configuration (.graphqlrc.json):**
```json
{
  "schema": "https://localhost:5001/graphql",
  "documents": "**/*.graphql",
  "extensions": {
    "strawberryShake": {
      "name": "ProtectionClient",
      "namespace": "ECO.Protection.GraphQL.Client",
      "url": "https://localhost:5001/graphql",
      "dependencyInjection": true
    }
  }
}
```

**Generated C# Client:**
```csharp
// Auto-generated C# client
public interface IProtectionClient
{
    Task<IOperationResult<IGetApplicationsResult>> GetApplicationsAsync(
        string stationName,
        string[]? devicePtns,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);

    Task<IOperationResult<ICreateApplicationsResult>> CreateApplicationsAsync(
        CreateApplicationInput[] input,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
}

// Usage in C# services
public class ApplicationOrchestrator
{
    private readonly IProtectionClient _client;

    public ApplicationOrchestrator(IProtectionClient client)
    {
        _client = client;
    }

    public async Task<DeviceApplication[]> GetDevicesAsync(string stationName)
    {
        var result = await _client.GetApplicationsAsync(stationName, null);

        if (result.Errors?.Any() == true)
        {
            throw new GraphQLException(result.Errors);
        }

        return result.Data.Applications.Data.ToArray();
    }
}
```

#### GraphQL Operations File Organization

Organize GraphQL queries/mutations in separate `.graphql` files for better maintainability:

```
src/
├── app/
│   ├── graphql/
│   │   ├── queries/
│   │   │   ├── get-applications.graphql
│   │   │   ├── get-device-information.graphql
│   │   │   └── get-station-hierarchy.graphql
│   │   ├── mutations/
│   │   │   ├── create-applications.graphql
│   │   │   ├── update-device-information.graphql
│   │   │   └── delete-applications.graphql
│   │   └── fragments/
│   │       ├── device-fields.graphql
│   │       ├── function-group-fields.graphql
│   │       └── validation-error-fields.graphql
│   └── generated/
│       └── graphql.ts  # Auto-generated from above files
```

**Example - get-applications.graphql:**
```graphql
#import "../fragments/device-fields.graphql"
#import "../fragments/validation-error-fields.graphql"

query GetApplications($stationName: String!, $devicePtns: [String!]) {
  applications(stationName: $stationName, devicePtns: $devicePtns) {
    data {
      ...DeviceFields
    }
    errors {
      ...ValidationErrorFields
    }
  }
}
```

**Example - device-fields.graphql (Fragment):**
```graphql
fragment DeviceFields on DeviceApplication {
  id
  publicTechnicalName
  deviceName
  orderNumber
  deviceType
  functionGroups {
    id
    name
    functions {
      name
      type
      enabled
    }
  }
  createdAt
  updatedAt
}
```

#### CI/CD Integration

**1. NPM Script (package.json):**
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "graphql:generate": "graphql-codegen --config codegen.yml",
    "graphql:watch": "graphql-codegen --config codegen.yml --watch",
    "prebuild": "npm run graphql:generate"
  }
}
```

**2. Build Pipeline (Azure DevOps):**
```yaml
# azure-pipelines.yml
trigger:
  branches:
    include:
      - main
      - develop

stages:
  - stage: Build
    jobs:
      - job: Frontend
        steps:
          - task: NodeTool@0
            inputs:
              versionSpec: '20.x'
            displayName: 'Install Node.js'

          - script: npm install
            displayName: 'Install dependencies'

          - script: npm run graphql:generate
            displayName: 'Generate GraphQL types'
            env:
              GRAPHQL_ENDPOINT: $(GraphQLEndpoint)

          - script: npm run build
            displayName: 'Build Angular app'

          - script: npm test
            displayName: 'Run tests'
```

**3. Pre-commit Hook (Husky):**
```json
// package.json
{
  "husky": {
    "hooks": {
      "pre-commit": "npm run graphql:generate && git add src/generated/graphql.ts"
    }
  }
}
```

#### Schema Change Detection

**Workflow when backend schema changes:**

1. **Backend Developer** updates GraphQL schema (e.g., adds new field to `DeviceApplication`)
2. **Backend CI/CD** deploys to dev environment
3. **Frontend Developer** runs `npm run graphql:generate`
4. **GraphQL Codegen** detects schema changes:
   ```
   ✔ Parse configuration
   ✔ Generate outputs
   ℹ Schema changed: New field 'serialNumber' added to DeviceApplication
   ```
5. **TypeScript Compiler** shows compile errors if queries need updates:
   ```
   ERROR in src/app/components/device-list.component.ts:45:23
   Property 'serialNumber' does not exist on type 'DeviceApplication'
   ```
6. **Developer** updates queries to use new field (or ignores if not needed)
7. **Commit** includes both query updates and generated type updates

**Breaking Change Detection:**
```bash
# Compare schema versions
npx graphql-inspector diff \
  https://localhost:5001/graphql \  # New schema
  ./schema-baseline.graphql          # Previous schema

# Output:
# ✖ Field 'DeviceApplication.deviceName' changed type from 'String' to 'String!'
# ✖ Argument 'stationName' removed from field 'Query.applications'
# ✔ 2 breaking changes detected
```

#### Comparison: Manual DTOs vs Code Generation

| Aspect | Manual DTOs (Before) | Code Generation (After) |
|--------|---------------------|------------------------|
| **Type Safety** | Runtime errors only | Compile-time errors |
| **Maintenance** | Manual updates when backend changes | Auto-updated with `npm run graphql:generate` |
| **Sync Issues** | Frontend DTOs can drift from backend | Always synchronized with schema |
| **Development Time** | ~2 hours to write/update 20 DTOs | ~5 minutes for codegen setup, then automatic |
| **Error Prone** | High (typos, missing fields, wrong types) | Low (generated code is always correct) |
| **Documentation** | Separate docs needed | Types include JSDoc from schema |
| **Refactoring** | Manual search/replace across codebase | IDE refactoring works correctly |
| **Onboarding** | New devs must learn DTO structure | Auto-complete guides new devs |
| **API Changes** | Silent failures if DTOs not updated | Build breaks if queries incompatible |

**Before (Manual DTOs):**
```typescript
// ❌ Manual DTO - must be kept in sync with backend
export interface DeviceApplication {
  publicTechnicalName: string;
  deviceName: string;  // ❌ Wrong! Backend allows null
  orderNumber: string;
  functionGroups: FunctionGroup[];  // ❌ What if backend changes this?
}

// ❌ API call with no type safety
getApplications(stationName: string): Observable<any> {
  return this.http.get(`/api/applications/${stationName}`).pipe(
    map((response: any) => response.data)  // ❌ No type checking
  );
}
```

**After (Code Generation):**
```typescript
// ✅ Auto-generated types - always match backend
import { DeviceApplication, GetApplicationsGQL } from '../generated/graphql';

**Status**: Draft / In Review / Approved
